---
import HagueDoc from '../../../layouts/HagueDoc.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    q: 'Do I need an Apostille to sell, buy or register a home in China while I am in Canada?',
    a: 'Yes. After Ontario notarization, the Power of Attorney must receive an Apostille from Ontario Official Documents Services. Housing bureaus in China generally will not accept a notary stamp alone. Call 647-930-6688.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What powers should the POA include?',
    a: 'Whatever the local registry requires: signing, filing, collecting the title certificate, paying tax, handling a mortgage, and so on. Missing one item usually means a rewrite and a new Apostille. Miao He checks the local template before drafting.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I really pick up the original the next day?',
    a: 'Urgent files can be scheduled for next-day pickup of the Apostille original if the text is right, identity documents are complete, and the ODS window is available. Recent China-property POAs were collected the next day and accepted on the first filing. Call 647-930-6688 to confirm the same-day slot.',
  },
  {
    q: 'What if each city in China uses a different form?',
    a: 'Ask the local housing bureau or real-estate registry for their template first. If there is none, Miao He drafts a Chinese–English POA to the usual local requirements. A wrong grant of authority or property description will be rejected in China even with a valid Apostille.',
  },
];
---
<HagueDoc lang="en" slug="china-property-power-of-attorney" faqs={faqs}>
  <div class="definition-box">
    <p>Miao He recently apostilled <strong>Powers of Attorney for selling, buying and registering title to property in China</strong>. The drafts followed the receiving office’s format, were <strong>accepted on the first pass — no supplementary materials, no rewrites</strong> — and clients <strong>signed once at the office</strong>, with no separate translator or Notary Public. Urgent clients <strong>picked up the Apostille original the next day</strong>. If you need it quickly, this is the file to bring here.</p>
  </div>

  <h2>When you need this POA</h2>
  <p>You are in Canada. The property is in China. Someone there has to sign, file or collect a title certificate for you. Typical files:</p>
  <ul class="content-list">
    <li>selling and transferring a home, paying tax, receiving sale proceeds;</li>
    <li>buying, completing a mortgage, and registering title;</li>
    <li>a purchase already completed, with only title registration left;</li>
    <li>discharging a mortgage, replacing a certificate, or a name change on the register.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>The window in China looks at who you are, what the agent may do, which property, and whether Chinese and English names match. Miao He is a <strong>dual-licensed China–Canada litigation lawyer</strong>. She drafts the authority, the property description and the identity details so they survive that review.</p>

  <h2>Why many POAs are rejected in China</h2>
  <p>The notary step was done. The text was wrong: authority too vague, no power to collect the certificate or pay tax, a wrong title number, or a passport name that does not match the deed. Changing the wording after the Apostille means starting over. Miao He writes to the receiving office’s format first, then notarizes, then apostilles — which is why these recent property POAs went through on the first filing.</p>
  <p>If the name on your passport, Chinese ID and property deed are not the same spelling, you often also need a <a href="/en/hague-apostille/same-name-declaration/">same-name declaration</a>. Otherwise the POA can be apostilled and still stall on “is this the same person.”</p>

  <h2>What to bring</h2>
  <ul class="content-list">
    <li>passport and Canadian ID;</li>
    <li>the China title certificate, purchase contract or registry extract;</li>
    <li>the agent’s ID details;</li>
    <li>the local bureau’s template, if they have one;</li>
    <li>former-name, hukou or old-ID copies if the names do not match.</li>
  </ul>
</HagueDoc>
